00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:21.420
I'm Tony Hawk, pro skater.

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:23.220
I've been skating the last 22 years of my life,

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:24.560
the last 18 as a professional.

00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:26.950
And the question I get asked most often is,

00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:28.830
how do you learn to skate? What are the basics?

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:29.980
Well, we're here to take...

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.040
through them with the help of some other pro skaters, and we want to get you started with

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:36.610
all the basics of skating, so let's start skating.

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:28.340
All right, let's talk about the basics of street skating.

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:29.980
We want to take you through the elusive.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:33.030
of ollie and 180s and pop shove-its.

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:36.150
And once you're taking it through the ollies, the 180s, and the shove-its, we're going to

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:37.930
teach you kickflips and heel flips.

00:04:38.890 --> 00:04:39.670
So let's get started.

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:40.470
Let's go street skating.

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:53.070
The most important trick you're going to learn in skating is the ollie.

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:57.200
And the ollie is the act of jumping up, using only your feet, and bringing the board up

00:04:57.200 --> 00:04:58.590
with you without grabbing it.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.960
The key elements to the Ollie are timing, timing, timing, timing, and your weight distribution

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:07.020
and your foot placement.

00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:12.210
So basically what you want to do is kick down on your back foot so that it just snaps the

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:16.420
tail down, and as your board comes up you slide your front foot forward in a rolling

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:16.840
motion.

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:21.460
That'll bring it level up to your feet, you're in the air, your knees are bent, and you land

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:22.280
it with all four wheels.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.380
Once you're pretty confident with your ollies, we can talk about 180 ollies.

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:37.680
180 ollies are basically half a turn with your body and your board in the air as you

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:38.000
ollie.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:41.060
One of the first ways to learn it is by doing it frontside.

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:45.650
Frontside means you're turning towards your front heel and you can see the ollie the whole

00:05:45.650 --> 00:05:46.090
way around.

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:52.280
Basically you're winding up your shoulders and ollieing and as you ollie your lower body

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:56.160
will follow your shoulders and as you come around if you're not turned completely you

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:59.100
You can kind of use your feet to adjust that and land on your front wheels first.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.400
sort of drive it around. Back to 180s, basically the same thing, but you're turning your shoulder

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:10.500
the other way, going blind, and same. If you're not around the whole way, you can use your

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:14.700
front feet, land, pivot around, and you've got 180s. It doesn't necessarily mean one's

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:19.060
easier than the other. I've seen people learn one before the other. It just depends on what

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:23.800
you're comfortable with. Just basically try both, see which one feels better. Let's go.

00:06:32.170 --> 00:06:38.870
The biggest step in skating a mini-ramp is trying to figure out how to drop in on it.

00:06:39.050 --> 00:06:41.570
And it may look scary when you're first up here, but we're going to show you the safest

00:06:41.570 --> 00:06:42.350
way to go about it.

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:43.140
So let's do it.

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:54.620
One thing you want to remember with dropping in, so make sure you put your weight on your

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:55.680
front foot as you go down.

00:06:55.840 --> 00:06:58.140
If you hesitate, that's when you get in trouble.

00:06:58.370 --> 00:06:59.850
So just make sure you put your foot on the right...

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:03.120
Right place, right over the bolts, and commit to it.

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:05.040
Drop in, go. Don't hesitate.

00:07:08.180 --> 00:07:11.180
As your front wheels touch down, your weight shifts to your front leg,

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:13.840
and as you descend, you want to even out that weight

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:15.720
and absorb the transition with your knees.

00:07:18.590 --> 00:07:21.870
If you happen to fall because your weight is too far forward or backwards,

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:25.380
compensate on the next attempt so that you stay centered over your board.

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:29.980
The biggest mistake with dropping in is to hit...

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:34.280
hesitate and not put your weight on the front foot, and Chris, you want to demonstrate that

00:07:34.280 --> 00:07:34.640
for us?

00:07:35.380 --> 00:07:36.240
No, actually no.

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:36.960
Why don't you do it?

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:38.660
Why don't you take the slam for once?

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:39.640
Okay.

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:41.480
Here's what not to do when dropping in.

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:45.160
Once you put your foot down, make sure your weight's on it, otherwise you're going to go

00:07:45.160 --> 00:07:45.540
like this.

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:53.130
So make sure that your weight's on your front foot as you go down.

